Newcastle's FA Cup campaign began in the third round against fellow Premier League side [[Everton F.C.|Everton]]. The only goal of the match came in the second half; [[John Beresford (footballer)|John Beresford]]'s right-wing cross beat the Everton defence and allowed [[John Barnes (footballer)|John Barnes]] to hook the ball back from beyond the far post.<ref name="everton">{{cite news |title=Football: Rush goal rubs salt into Everton's wounds |first=Guy |last=Hodgson |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-rush-goal-rubs-salt-into-evertons-wounds-1136836.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-rush-goal-rubs-salt-into-evertons-wounds-1136836.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=5 January 1998 |access-date=3 June 2012}}{{cbignore}}</ref> [[Ian Rush]] forced the ball over the line, and in the process set a new goalscoring record – scoring 43 goals in the competition.<ref name="everton"/> Newcastle were drawn away to non-league [[Stevenage F.C.|Stevenage Borough]] in the fourth round. [[Alan Shearer]] on his return scored inside the first three minutes; he headed the ball into the net after good play from [[Keith Gillespie]].<ref name="stev">{{cite news |title=Football: Grazioli keeps Stevenage under the spotlight |first=Phil |last=Shaw |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-grazioli-keeps-stevenage-under-the-spotlight-1140932.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-grazioli-keeps-stevenage-under-the-spotlight-1140932.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=26 January 1998 |access-date=3 June 2012}}{{cbignore}}</ref> [[Giuliano Grazioli]] equalised for the home side and they held on for a replay at [[St James' Park]].<ref name="stev"/>

In the rematch, Shearer scored both goals against Stevenage Borough. His manager [[Kenny Dalglish]] was irritated at the lack of courtesy his opponents had shown, and said afterwards: "Off the pitch they have a lot to learn about manners. If they were a Premier League club they would probably have been done for bringing the game into disrepute."<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer double sees off Stevenage |first=Michael |last=Walker |page=22 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=5 February 1998}}</ref> A 1–0 win in the fifth round at home to [[Tranmere Rovers]] was followed by a quarter-final draw against [[Barnsley F.C.|Barnsley]], who had knocked out competition favourites [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Schmeichel slip-up wrecks treble dream |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/60186.stm |work=BBC News |date=26 February 1998 |access-date=3 June 2012}}</ref> Newcastle advanced to the semi-finals after a 3–1 win and at [[Old Trafford]], Shearer scored the winner against [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Newcastle don Sunday best to go through |first=Oliver |last=Holt |page=30 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=9 March 1998}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Football: Shearer's strike breaks the Blades|first=Glenn |last=Moore |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-shearers-strike-breaks-the-blades-1154754.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-shearers-strike-breaks-the-blades-1154754.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=6 April 1998 |access-date=3 June 2012}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
Arsenal were appearing in the final of the FA Cup for the first time since [[1993 FA Cup final|1993]].<ref name="cupf">{{cite web |url=http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/history/cup-finals |title=Complete cup finals for Arsenal |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=5 November 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304082911/http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/history/cup-finals |archive-date=4 March 2016 |df=dmy-all }}</ref> They had won the cup on six occasions (in [[1930 FA Cup final|1930]], [[1936 FA Cup final|1936]], [[1950 FA Cup final|1950]], [[1971 FA Cup final|1971]], [[1979 FA Cup final|1979]] and 1993) and lost the showpiece final by the same amount.<ref name="cupf"/> Newcastle in contrast were making their first Cup final appearance in 24 years.<ref name="new">{{cite web |url=http://www.statto.com:80/football/teams/newcastle-united/history/cup-finals |title=Complete cup finals for Newcastle United |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=5 November 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160311080405/http://www.statto.com/football/teams/newcastle-united/history/cup-finals |archive-date=11 March 2016 |df=dmy-all }}</ref> The club had won six FA Cups, and came runners-up five times, most recently against [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in [[1974 FA Cup final|1974]].<ref name="new"/>
[[File:Wembleyold.jpg|thumb|225px|The final was held at Wembley Stadium, where both sides had faced each other twice before in the competition.]]
The 1998 final marked the third meeting between Arsenal and Newcastle United in Cup final history.<ref name="indyf">{{cite news |title=FA Cup Final: Routes to the final |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/education/education-news/football-fa-cup-final-routes-to-the-final-1159439.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/education/education-news/football-fa-cup-final-routes-to-the-final-1159439.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=15 May 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Newcastle were victorious on both occasions: the first match in 1932 ended 2–1 in their favour, while the second, staged twenty years later, was settled by a single goal by [[George Robledo]].<ref name="indyf"/> The recent head-to-head record between the clubs was vastly different. In Dalglish's first full season at Newcastle, his team struggled for consistency and spent much of the [[Premier League]] campaign hovering in mid-table. They lost both matches against Arsenal, 1–0 at home in December 1997 and 3–0 away in April 1998.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wright reaps collective benefit |first=David |last=Hopps |page=20 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=8 December 1997 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Gunning for United |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/77186.stm |work=BBC News |date=12 April 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> It marked a turnaround in fortune as Newcastle for the past two seasons had competed intensely with Manchester United for the league title. Arsenal on the other hand hit their stride in the final months of the season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Title run-in: United steel or Arsenal grit? |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/77544.stm |work=BBC News |date=30 April 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> The team, managed by [[Arsène Wenger]], had made up a sizeable gap on Manchester United to win the title.<ref>{{cite news |title=Total recall, 1997–98 |url=https://www.arsenal.com/news/features/20140423/1997/98 |publisher=Arsenal F.C |date=23 April 2014 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> In doing so, Wenger became the first non-British manager to win the league championship in England.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal revel in success of Wenger's French evolution |first=Oliver |last=Holt |page=25 |newspaper=The Times |date=4 May 1998}}</ref>

Arsenal were favourites to win the FA Cup, and on course to complete an "unlikely" [[double (association football)|double]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Gunners' glory |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/86560.stm |work=BBC News |date=3 May 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> The fitness of Bergkamp, who was instrumental in Arsenal's successful season, was a major story in the days leading up to the final. The striker had limped out of their game against [[Derby County F.C.|Derby County]] in late April, and after it emerged he pulled his hamstring Bergkamp was ruled out for the remainder of Arsenal's league campaign.<ref>{{cite news |title=Premiership D-Day looms |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/86429.stm |work=BBC News |date=1 May 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> Wenger revealed two days before the final that Bergkamp had resumed training and "medically he looked all right", but the player received a further setback when he suffered a reaction to his hamstring.<ref name="davmi">{{cite news |title=Football: I've dreamed of this all my life, but I can't take the risk. |url=https://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+I%27ve+dreamed+of+this+all+my+life%2C+but+I+can%27t+take+the+risk.-a060678407 |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=16 May 1998 |access-date=6 November 2017}}</ref> He ruled himself out of the match and told reporters: "I'm very disappointed. As a little boy in Holland I always watched the FA Cup final. And I used to dream about playing in it myself one day."<ref name="davmi"/> [[Ian Wright]] was listed as Bergkamp's probable replacement to partner Anelka.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bergkamp blurs visions of double |first=Oliver |last=Holt |page=33 |newspaper=The Times |date=16 May 1998}}</ref>

Newcastle had their own issues surrounding Shearer. The England international was involved in a tangle with [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] player [[Neil Lennon]] during a league match, which went unnoticed by the referee.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer apologises for 'kick' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/86318.stm |work=BBC News |date=30 April 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> Television cameras showed Shearer kicking Lennon in the face, something he denied was premeditated.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer facing trial by video |first=David |last=Maddock |page=56 |newspaper=The Times |date=1 May 1998}}</ref> He was charged with misconduct,<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer charged with misconduct |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/88739.stm |work=BBC News |date=7 May 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> but escaped a ban from the FA's disciplinary panel.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shearer cleared in boot row |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/91721.stm |work=BBC News |date=12 May 1998 |access-date=5 November 2017}}</ref> Shearer's teammate [[Rob Lee]] felt the striker was in the right shape of mind to face Arsenal, saying: "He's too strong a person to have let that affect him, even if it hadn't been sorted."<ref>{{cite news |title=Shear venom |url=https://www.thefreelibrary.com/SHEAR+VENOM.-a060678292 |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=15 May 1998 |access-date=6 November 2017}}</ref> Dalglish talked before the game of Newcastle's disappointing league campaign and questioned "why we couldn't translate our Cup results into the league".<ref name="dalg">{{cite news |title=Waiting to have the last laugh |first=Oliver |last=Holt |page=35 |newspaper=The Times |date=16 May 1998}}</ref> He dismissed talk of Newcastle having an "easy run" in the competition, saying "It's not our fault if our opponents beat teams who were favourites to go through."<ref name="dalg"/>

Continuing a tradition which began in the 1970s, the finalists each released a song as part of the buildup to the final. The Arsenal squad recorded a cover of [[Donna Summer]]'s "[[Hot Stuff (Donna Summer song)|Hot Stuff]]" with proceeds going to the Arsenal Charitable Trust,<ref>{{cite news |title=Wright shows he's still an Arsenal hit |page=64 |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=1 May 1998}}</ref> while Newcastle enlisted celebrity supporter [[Sting (musician)|Sting]] to write "Black and White Army (Bringing the Pride Back Home)" (sang by [[Ryan Molloy]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=Ryan Malloy: Black & White Army |url=http://www.sting.com/discography/album/262/albums |publisher=Sting |access-date=22 April 2018}}</ref> Arsenal's song spent seven weeks in the [[UK Singles Chart]] and peaked at number nine.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arsenal F.C |url=http://www.officialcharts.com/artist/28454/arsenal-f-c/ |website=Official Charts Company |access-date=6 November 2017}}</ref> Royalty was present at Wembley on the day of the final; [[Prince Edward, Duke of Kent]] was on hand to perform various duties alongside his wife [[Katharine, Duchess of Kent]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Royal engagements |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/obituaries/royal-engagements-1159414.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/obituaries/royal-engagements-1159414.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=15 May 1998 |access-date=6 November 2017}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Players from both sides were adorned in [[Hugo Boss]] suits for their customary pre-match walk.<ref>{{cite news |first=Tasmin |last=Blanchard |title=Fashion victims |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/the-fashion-victims-of-football-1159362.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/the-fashion-victims-of-football-1159362.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=15 May 1998 |access-date=6 November 2017}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
Wenger selected Wreh to lead Arsenal's attack, and left Wright on the substitutes’ bench. The Liberian striker partnered Anelka in a front two, and they were supported by wide midfielders Overmars and Parlour. Dalglish named [[Alessandro Pistone]] in the starting eleven to play as right back; commentator [[John Motson]] suggested the defender's role was to nullify Overmars.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the first half, coverage between 10:52pm and 10:53pm. "There's a feeling that Pistone will play at right back to mark Marc Overmars, with Warren Barton playing in front of him."</ref> [[Temuri Ketsbaia]] was picked ahead of [[Andreas Andersson (footballer, born 1974)|Andreas Andersson]] and [[John Barnes (footballer)|John Barnes]] started the game on the bench. Rush did not make the 16-man squad for Newcastle, as he failed to recover from injury. Both clubs were set  up in a traditional  [[Formation (association football)#4–4–2|4–4–2]] system: a four-man [[Defender (association football)|defence]] (comprising two [[centre-back]]s and [[Full-back (association football)|left and right full-backs]]), four [[midfielder]]s (two in the [[Central midfielder|centre]], and one on each [[Wide midfielder|wing]]) and two [[centre-forward]]s.

Newcastle kicked-off the final on a warm Saturday afternoon and quickly set about testing Arsenal by aiming long balls in their half. Arsenal won many of the aerial duels and broke quickly once they had possession.<ref name="mots">Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the first half, coverage between 10:54pm and 10:56pm.</ref> Parlour chased after a long ball by Dixon from the right, but Howey impeded the midfielder and put the ball out for a corner.<ref name="mots"/> It came to nothing as the whistle was blown once Adams's climbed above his opponent.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the first half, coverage between 10:56pm and 10:57pm.</ref> Newcastle's first attempt on goal was created by Ketsbaia, who won the ball near the centre of the pitch. He shifted it onto his left foot, but his shot from about {{convert|20|yd}} bobbled past Arsenal goalkeeper [[David Seaman]]'s left-hand post.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the first half, coverage between 10:58pm and 10:59pm.</ref> Arsenal's first real chance was a move which began from the backline. Vieira found Parlour who kicked the ball towards Dixon. The Arsenal right-back saw Parlour making a dart forward into the opposition's penalty area and returned the ball to the midfielder.<ref name="arseweb">{{cite web |title=Arsenal vs Newcastle. FA Cup Final 16/05/98 |url=http://www.arseweb.com/97-98/reports/160598.html |work=Arseweb |date=16 May 1998 |access-date=28 April 2018 |archive-date=29 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180429092625/http://www.arseweb.com/97-98/reports/160598.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Parlour's cross from the right was met by Anelka in the box, but the striker headed the ball over the post.<ref name="arseweb"/> Arsenal took the lead minutes later through Overmars. A through-ball by [[Emmanuel Petit]] had found the charging winger and he resisted the challenge of Pistone to prod the ball through the legs of the onrushing goalkeeper [[Shay Given]].<ref name="holt">{{cite news |title=Overmars provides the driving force |first=Oliver |last=Holt |page=31 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=18 May 1998}}</ref> It was Overmars' 16th goal of the campaign, his most prolific season as a professional footballer.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newvision.co.ug/new_vision/news/1314977/overmars-gareth-bale-reminds-thierry-henry |title=Overmars: Gareth Bale reminds me of Thierry Henry |newspaper=New Vision |location=Kampala |date=3 March 2013 |access-date=28 April 2018 |quote=I must say winning the double with Arsenal in my first season at the club (1997–1998). I remember I scored 16 goals that season which was my best overall.}}</ref> Newcastle struggled to breach Arsenal's stable defence for the remainder of the first half; Ketsbaia saw his curling shot on target but comfortably caught by Seaman.<ref name="brook">Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the first half, coverage between 11:02pm and 11:05pm.</ref> Overmars, a constant threat in the match, crossed the ball towards Parlour in the penalty area, but with room to shoot the midfielder skied it over.<ref name="brook"/> Newcastle appealed for a penalty deep into added time after Adams jumped over Shearer while looking to clear the ball, but the referee immediately turned it down.<ref name="brook"/>

In the second half, Newcastle began more adventurously and forced Arsenal into moving the ball at a higher tempo. From a free-kick awarded near the left channel, Lee crossed the ball and up rose [[Nikos Dabizas]], whose header hit the bar.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs in the second half, coverage between 11:10pm and 11:13pm.</ref> Newcastle were presented with another opportunity to equalise after Arsenal defender [[Martin Keown]] slipped on the ball. Shearer pounced, but his left-foot shot hit the foot of the post, and rebounded back out.<ref name="holt"/> Newcastle were punished for poor finishing in the 69th minute as Anelka scored for Arsenal. Parlour lifted the ball through to the teenage striker who controlled it with his chest.<ref name="holt"/> He raced past his marker Dabizas, and steered the ball into the bottom left-hand corner. Newcastle responded by bringing on Andersson, an extra forward, in place of Pearce but the change in personnel made no difference to the outcome.<ref name="arseweb"/> Parlour, later awarded man of the match, nearly scored a third for Arsenal, but his effort hit the side netting.<ref name="arseweb"/> As the whistle blew for full-time, Wenger hugged his assistant [[Pat Rice]] in the dugout before joining his players on the field for celebrations.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs at the end of the match, coverage between 11:22pm and 11:23pm.</ref> Sportingly, the Newcastle fans applauded the Arsenal team as they made their way up the Wembley steps to collect the trophy.<ref>Motson, Brooking (1998). Event occurs at the end of the match, coverage between 11:23pm and 11:25pm.</ref>

