==Post-match==
Arsenal's win had sealed a league and cup double and Wenger, the club's manager, described it as "the greatest moment of my sporting life."<ref name="postar">{{cite news |title=Wenger's great day |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S2 |date=17 May 1998}}</ref> Speaking to the media, he complimented his team for their performance and said of the season: "We kept getting better and so has the mixture between the foreign and English players."<ref name="postar"/> Wenger reserved praise for the goalscorers Overmars ("a world-class player") and Anelka ("Nicolas is very young. But you saw in the second half today what he can do"), and felt the turning point in the match was Shearer's shot hitting the post.<ref name="holt"/><ref name="postar"/> He sympathised with Newcastle, as did his captain, Adams who said afterwards: "The team and their fans did everything they could out there, but it was our day. It capped a fantastic season for us and was a day we'll never forget."<ref name="postar"/> Overmars called winning the FA Cup, "...the biggest thing that you can dream of. I'd say it's the same as winning the European Cup", while Dixon, a long-serving member of the team, added Arsenal's achievement had "...surpasse[d] anything that we've done in the past."<ref>{{cite news |title=Cup Final: 'Better than anything we've done' |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-cup-final-better-than-anything-weve-done-1158948.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-cup-final-better-than-anything-weve-done-1158948.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent on Sunday |location=London |date=16 May 1998 |access-date=25 April 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref>
[[File:Ray Parlour 2012.jpg|thumb|200px|upright|[[Ray Parlour]] (pictured in 2012) was awarded the man of the match prize.]]
Dalglish congratulated Arsenal on completing the double, as well as both sets of supporters: "I think it's the first time I've come to Wembley and seen both sets of fans applauding the winning side."<ref name="postnewc">{{cite news |title=Dalglish applauds fans |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S2 |date=17 May 1998}}</ref> He felt Newcastle's performance improved in the second half – "...our players seemed to have more belief in themselves", though concluded despite the efforts it "just wasn't to be."<ref name="postnewc"/> Pearce agreed with his manager that Arsenal deserved the win and told reporters: "We failed to deliver and our magnificent fans deserved better. We'll have to come back stronger next season."<ref name="postnewc"/>
{{Quote box|width=250px|bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |align=left|quote="Arsène Wenger may have dressed like an undertaker for Wembley, but he is a Prince of Light for the English game."|salign = left|source= Rob Hughes' assessment of Arsenal's manager in ''The Times'', 18 May 1998<ref name="hughes">{{cite news| first=Rob |last=Hughes |title=Wenger leading English game towards exit from insularity |newspaper=The Times |location=London |page=31 |date=18 May 1998}}</ref>}}
Journalists and pundits reviewing the final unanimously agreed that Arsenal were deserved winners. [[Ian Ridley]] wrote in ''[[The Independent on Sunday]]'' of 17 May 1998: "Where Arsenal played legato, Newcastle were staccato in their passing and though they enjoyed a purple patch after raising the tempo in the second half, twice hitting the goal frame, they were largely limp opposition."<ref>{{cite news|first=Ian |last=Ridley |title=Cup Final: The Double cream team |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-cup-final-the-double-cream-team-1158947.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-cup-final-the-double-cream-team-1158947.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Independent on Sunday |location=London |date=17 May 1998 |access-date=25 April 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref> ''[[The Guardian]]'' football correspondent [[David Lacey]] was in awe of the double winners' performance, writing "There have been more distinguished Wembley triumphs but it is hard to remember a final being won with the sheet pace of Arsenal's victory over Newcastle on Saturday."<ref>{{cite news| first=David |last=Lacey |title=Arsenal simply red-hot |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |page=A1 |date=18 May 1998}}</ref> Paul Wilson of ''[[The Observer]]'' believed in comparison to their title charge it was "lukewarm stuff",<ref>{{cite news| first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Too hot for the Toon |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |page=B3 |date=17 May 1998}}</ref> while ''[[The Sunday Times]]'' match reporter Joe Lovejoy felt the London club had got the better of Newcastle in what was a "pretty ordinary game".<ref name="lovj">{{cite news| first=Joe |last=Lovejoy |title=Red-hot Arsenal cruise to Double |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S1 |date=17 May 1998}}</ref> Lovejoy did however credit the Newcastle support – "The Toon Army won the decibel count, which was no surprise."<ref name="lovj"/> [[David Pleat]] wrote in his analysis column for ''The Sunday Times'' that key to Arsenal's victory was winning the midfield battle. He singled the "prodigious" Parlour out for praise, writing "nobody did more efficient work than the man rejuvenated at Arsenal."<ref>{{cite news| first=David |last=Pleat |title=Power play scatters the Magpies |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S3 |date=17 May 1998}}</ref>

The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] and [[Sky Sports]]; the former provided the free-to-air coverage and Sky Sports 2 was the pay-TV alternative.<ref>{{cite news |title=One-nil to the ITV |first=Viv |last=Hardwick |page=10 |newspaper=The Northern Echo |location=Darlington |date=16 May 1998}}</ref> Highlights were also shown on ''[[Match of the Day]]'' that evening on [[BBC One]]. ITV held the majority of the viewership, with an overnight audience of 7.8 million viewers (65% viewing share), whereas 900,000 watched on Sky.<ref name="ratings">{{cite news |title=Sunshine 2: TV-football 0 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/sunshine-2-tv-football-0-1158564.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/sunshine-2-tv-football-0-1158564.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=19 May 1998 |access-date=25 April 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref> The terrestrial figure was the lowest in Cup final history in more than a decade, which ITV attributed to the May heatwave.<ref name="ratings"/>

A day after the win Arsenal paraded both trophies on an open-top bus, heading towards [[Islington Town Hall]] for a civic reception.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal and Hearts take to the streets |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/95204.stm |work=BBC News |date=23 May 1998 |access-date=25 April 2018}}</ref> The club's victory set up a [[FA Community Shield|Charity Shield]] match against Manchester United, the league runners-up, in August 1998. As cup winners, Arsenal would have ordinarily been awarded a spot in the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]], but as the club qualified for the [[UEFA Champions League]] via their league position the place was passed to finalists Newcastle United.<ref>{{cite news |title=Newcastle United gloom deepens as chairman quits |first1=Lisa |last1=Buckingham |first2=Tony |last2=May |page=25 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=28 May 1998}}</ref>

==Aftermath and legacy==
Arsenal were unable to retain the FA Cup the following season as the team fell to Manchester United at the semi-final stage.<ref>{{cite news |title=Giggs magic sinks Gunners |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/fa_cup/319696.stm |work=BBC News |date=14 April 1999 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref> It was not until [[2001 FA Cup final|2001]] that they appeared in another Cup final, where [[Michael Owen]] scored twice at Cardiff's [[Millennium Stadium]] to hand [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] the trophy.<ref>{{cite news |title=Owen shatters Arsenal in Cup final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1324699.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref> Arsenal regrouped the following year, starting a run of three FA Cup wins in four years.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup winners list: Full record of finals and results from history |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/05/27/fa-cup-winners-list-full-record-finals-results-history/ |work=The Telegraph |date=27 May 2017 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref>  By [[2015 FA Cup final|2015]], Wenger had led Arsenal to a record-breaking 13th FA Cup and victory against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in [[2017 FA Cup final|2017]] earnt him the accolade of being the most successful manager in the competition's history.<ref>{{cite news |first=James |last=Walker-Roberts |title=Arsenal 4–0 Aston Villa: Dominant Gunners seal record FA Cup victory |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-a-villa/334699 |publisher=Sky Sports |date=31 May 2015 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39984089 |title=Arsenal 2–1 Chelsea |publisher=BBC Sport |date=27 May 2017 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref>

The 1998 Cup final was the climax to an eventful season for Wenger, who became the first manager born outside of the British Isles to complete the double.<ref>{{cite news |first=Tom |last=McGowan |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2018/04/20/football/arsene-wenger-out-arsenal-spt/index.html |title=Arsenal: 'Wenger Out' campaign finally gets its wish
 |work=CNN |date=20 April 2018 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref> As well as revitalising the club, his style of management was heralded as revolutionary.<ref>{{cite news |first=Rory |last=Smith |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/20/sports/soccer/arsene-wenger-arsenal.html |title=Arsène Wenger will leave Arsenal, proudly but imperfectly
 |newspaper=The New York Times |date=20 April 2018 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref> Rob Hughes in his ''Times'' column of 18 May 1998 credited the Arsenal manager for demonstrating "what there is to learn from overseas", and for blending players of different ages and cultural backgrounds into a successful squad.<ref name="hughes"/> The 1998 double-winning team is regarded as one of English football's finest; former Manchester United winger [[Ryan Giggs]] later described them as one of his toughest opponents: "They just had a bit of everything really. Quality with Bergkamp, pace with Anelka and Overmars, the experience of the back four, and then the toughness of Vieira and Petit in midfield."<ref>{{cite news |title=The Arsenal of 1997–98 and now: how the sides measure up |first=Toby |last=Moses |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/nov/08/arsenal-1998-compared-to-current-team |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=8 November 2013 |access-date=3 December 2017}}</ref>

For Newcastle, success was hard to come by. In August 1998, just two matches into the Premier League season, Dalglish was dismissed, replaced by former Chelsea manager [[Ruud Gullit]].<ref>{{cite news |title=The Arsenal of 1997–98 and now: how the sides measure up |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1999/aug/28/newsstory.sport6 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=28 August 1999 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref> Although the team reached another FA Cup final in [[1999 FA Cup final|1999]], they were again losing finalists.<ref>{{cite news |title=Speed hungry for FA Cup glory |url=https://www.walesonline.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/speed-hungry-fa-cup-glory-2406659 |publisher=Wales Online |date=18 April 2005 |access-date=28 April 2018}}</ref>

